---
layout: null
---
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
{%- comment -%}
  Hand-written RSS 2.0. jekyll-feed is not installed: its generator skips when the
  source already contains a feed.xml, so it never ran, and it emits Atom rather
  than the RSS 2.0 existing subscribers are on.

  <description> carries the post's summary, not its full rendered HTML. It used to
  be `post.content | xml_escape` for ten posts, which made this document hundreds
  of kilobytes of escaped markup — and useless markup at that, because the image
  paths inside it are page-relative (`../assets/images/...`). A feed reader
  resolves those against its own base, not the post's, so every image in the feed
  was broken. Summaries sidestep the whole problem and readers click through for
  the article, which is what the <link> is for.
{%- endcomment -%}
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom">
  <channel>
    <title>{{ site.title | xml_escape }}</title>
    <description>{{ site.description | xml_escape }}</description>
    <link>{{ '/' | absolute_url }}</link>
    <language>{{ site.lang | default: 'en' }}</language>
    <atom:link href="{{ '/feed.xml' | absolute_url }}" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/>
    <pubDate>{{ site.time | date_to_rfc822 }}</pubDate>
    <lastBuildDate>{{ site.time | date_to_rfc822 }}</lastBuildDate>
    <generator>Jekyll v{{ jekyll.version }}</generator>
    {%- comment -%}
      The same `hidden` filter index.html, search.json, /topics/, /archive/, the
      category pages, post-nav.html and series-nav.html all apply. Without it the
      feed was the one public surface still publishing unlisted drafts.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign feed_posts = site.posts | where_exp: "p", "p.hidden != true" -%}
    {% for post in feed_posts limit: 10 %}
      <item>
        <title>{{ post.title | xml_escape }}</title>
        {%- comment -%}
          `description` first — every post has one, written for exactly this job.
          The excerpt fallback is stripped of HTML because it would otherwise carry
          the same relative image paths described above.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <description>{{ post.description | default: post.excerpt | strip_html | strip_newlines | truncate: 400 | xml_escape }}</description>
        <pubDate>{{ post.date | date_to_rfc822 }}</pubDate>
        <link>{{ post.url | absolute_url }}</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">{{ post.url | absolute_url }}</guid>
        {%- comment -%}
          Categories only. A second loop over post.tags lived here too and emitted
          nothing for years: no post on this site has ever set `tags:`.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- for cat in post.categories %}
        <category>{{ cat | xml_escape }}</category>
        {%- endfor %}
      </item>
    {% endfor %}
  </channel>
</rss>
